    "api_error_status": null,
    "result": "`search_products` is implemented in `solution.py` and the suite in `tests/test_solution.py` passes: `python -m pytest -q tests` reports 31 passed. The implementation filters stock > 0, matches names with `instr(lower(name), lower(?))` so `%` and `_` stay literal and case folding is ASCII-only, applies `price <= max_price` when given, and orders by `name` or `price` (binary order for names) with `id` ascending as the tie-break regardless of `descending`. It validates `order_by` as an exact `\"name\"` or `\"price\"`, and `limit` and `offset` as true integers (bools rejected) within 1–100 and at least 0, raising `ValueError` otherwise. Each row is returned as a dict with exactly `id`, `name` and `price`, and only SELECT statements are issued. The tests cover each rule, the validation branches, pagination after filtering and ordering, and that the database is left unchanged. I could not measure line coverage because only the test command was allowed, so the 90% gate is unverified; by reading the code, every branch in `solution.py` is exercised by a test. Two of my own expected values in the tests were wrong on first run (\"fig\" contains \"g\"; the name-descending order was miscomputed), and I corrected them rather than changing the implementation.",
